# Ben-Or, Kelmer, Rabin 1994, "Asynchronous Secure Computations with
# Optimal Resilience", PODC '94, 183-192.  Extract: BKR94ACS.txt.
#
# Section 4, Protocol Agreement[Q], Figure 3 -- code for player Pi.
# n BAs run concurrently, one per player index, each player entering
# his inputs asynchronously as evidence accumulates:
#
#   1. For each Pj for whom you know that Q(j) = 1, participate in
#      BA_j with input 1.
#   2. Upon completing 2t+1 BA protocols with output 1, enter input 0
#      to all BA protocols for which you haven't entered a value yet.
#   3. Upon completing all n BA protocols, let your SubSet_i be the
#      set of all indices j for which BA_j had output 1.  Output
#      SubSet_i.
#
# From one seat: per j, whether Q(j) = 1 is known, whether this seat
# has entered its input to BA_j, and BA_j's output; across j, the count
# of BAs completed with output 1 and the count completed.
#
# Q(j) = 1 is "P_j has broadcast its value": this seat has accepted j's
# value by Figure 1 of Bracha87 (bracha87Fig1.dtc), one broadcast per
# player beside its BA.  The paper leaves Q to the application and asks
# two things of it.  That every honest player is eventually assigned 1
# is Bracha's Lemma 4; that an assigned Q(j) is eventually known to all
# is his Lemma 3; both hold while abandons and faults stay within t,
# and Lemma 4, where something rides beside, once it reaches the
# correct seats and passes their validation.  A faulty P_j is held to
# the same value at every correct seat that accepts or to none
# (Theorem 1), so a j in SubSet has one value.
# "Q(j) = 1 is known" is Figure 1's accept, set by the seat's C as
# Figure 3's "accepted" is.
# Each BA_j is Figure 4 of Bracha87 (bracha87Fig4.dtc): "enter input v
# to BA_j" is this seat broadcasting v at BA_j's first round, and
# "BA_j completed with output v" is Figure 4's decide for BA_j.  One
# run of these tables per event on the j the event is about: learning
# Q(j), BA_j deciding, or a sweep.
#
# Step 2's "upon completing 2t+1" is a wait, and the wait opens
# patience: a 0 entered for a laggard is a vote to exclude it from the
# subset, so the seat waits its patience past n - t before entering 0s,
# and need not wait at all once every BA has its input -- do not spend
# the Byzantine budget against a laggard.  The paper writes the count
# as 2t+1, the honest count n - t at n = 3t+1; the row reads it as the
# honest count, n - t, so that at t = 0 the subset is all players, or
# this seat outputs none: it abandons, or runs until its caller lets it
# go.
#
# Inputs:
#   "Q(j) = 1 is known"                { yes, no }
#   "my input to BA_j"                 { none, entered }
#   "BAs completed with output 1"      { < n-t, >= n-t }
#   "BAs completed"                    { < n, n }
#   "sweeps since n-t completed with output 1"  { < patience, >= patience }
#   "have output SubSet"               { yes, no }
#   "accepted the value of every j in SubSet"  { yes, no }  the caller's
#
# Outputs:
#   "enter 1 to BA_j"                  { yes, no }
#   "enter 0 to BA_j"                  { yes, no }
#   "output SubSet"                    { yes, no }  the j with output 1
#   "take step 3"                      { yes, no }  the caller's

# Step 1.
@"enter 1 to BA_j","Q(j) = 1 is known","my input to BA_j"
yes,yes,none
no,yes,entered
no,no,

# Step 2, past the patience, to a BA_j without this seat's input --
# and not one step 1 enters 1 to on this same run.
@"enter 0 to BA_j","enter 1 to BA_j","my input to BA_j","BAs completed with output 1","sweeps since n-t completed with output 1"
no,yes,,,
yes,no,none,>= n-t,>= patience
no,no,none,>= n-t,< patience
no,no,none,< n-t,
no,no,entered,,

# Step 3, once.
@"output SubSet","BAs completed","have output SubSet"
yes,n,no
no,n,yes
no,< n,

# ===== the caller's row =====

# Step 3 is taken when the paper says output and this seat has accepted
# the value of every j in SubSet: the output is the subset and its
# values together.  BA_j's output of 1 means some honest player entered
# 1, so it accepted j's value, and Lemma 3 eventually brings that
# accept here while this seat is correct; until it does the step
# waits, and abandon is the seat's exit.
@"take step 3","output SubSet","accepted the value of every j in SubSet"
yes,yes,yes
no,yes,no
no,no,
